linuxmuster-fileserver-verwaltung (7.3.0) lmn73; urgency=low

  * Initial release, derived from linuxmuster-fileserver (Netzint GmbH)
  * Target platform: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, linuxmuster.net 7.3
  * Provide a single "Verwaltung" share whose access control is managed
    entirely through Windows (NT ACLs in extended attributes)
  * Do not create any sophomorix-managed directory structure, so the data
    on this server is never rewritten by sophomorix-repair
  * Use the rid idmap backend instead of autorid: sophomorix writes no
    uidNumber/gidNumber into the AD, which rules out the ad/RFC2307 backend,
    and rid needs no persistent state to stay consistent across servers
  * Serve the share with "acl_xattr:ignore system acls = yes" so the NT ACL
    is the only authority and the POSIX-hash invalidation cannot silently
    discard the Windows permissions
  * Replace the "ntp" dependency of the upstream package, which in Ubuntu
    24.04 is only a universe transitional package pulling ntpsec, with
    "chrony | ntpsec | systemd-timesyncd"
  * Refuse to continue when no domain controller can be found, when the
    hostname exceeds the NetBIOS limit, or when the access group does not
    resolve - all three otherwise fail in ways that are hard to diagnose
  * Take the NetBIOS domain name from the CLDAP reply rather than assuming
    it is the first label of the DNS domain; getting this wrong would put
    every domain account into the tdb fallback range instead of the rid range
  * Quote every entry of "valid users" and "admin users": Samba splits list
    parameters on whitespace, so an unquoted "Domain Admins" would become two
    meaningless entries and lock out everyone, silently
  * Always include Domain Admins in "valid users" and always seed a baseline
    NT ACL, including when no access group is given - without either, not
    even an administrator could open the share to set permissions
  * Create --folder directories over SMB so they inherit the share ACL;
    created locally they would have no NT ACL and stay root:root 0755, which
    makes them invisible and unwritable
  * Record the SIDs of the access groups and compare them on every "status"
    run: a group that is deleted and recreated keeps its name but gets a new
    SID, which silently invalidates every NT ACL referencing it
  * Add "repair-acls" to re-establish the baseline ACL after such a change
  * Pass credentials to the Samba tools through a short-lived 0600 file
    instead of "-U user%password", which would expose them in the process list
  * Pin the locale of every Samba invocation: several checks match on English
    output that is translatable

 -- Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
